---
/**
 * SiteHeader — FIVE FLAT SEATS SINCE 2026-09-20 (D118), plus the full menu.
 *
 * Websites · Main Street · For manufacturers · Bio · Contact. No seat carries a
 * dropdown any more: the site sells three things and the row names them, with
 * the person and the way to reach him beside them. The <details> Menu stays as
 * the one complete list, because check-nav-coverage presumes a menu renders
 * every DESTINATIONS row and a page with no menu row is the class this file's
 * history records failing three times.
 *
 * CONTACT IS BACK BESIDE THE BUTTON, KNOWINGLY. The paragraph below headed WHY
 * CONTACT AND NOT ANOTHER SEAT records that this row gave the Contact seat up on
 * 2026-09-12 because the button beside it points at the same page. The operator
 * chose five seats with Contact among them on 2026-09-20 with that cost in front
 * of him; a visitor who only wants to ask a question now has a plain word to
 * press as well as a button asking for a project.
 *
 * MEASURE AFTER ANY LABEL CHANGE: `npm run measure:nav` against a running
 * preview, reading the HEADER CONTAINER and never the nav element. The reading
 * for these five is recorded beside the `nav` array below.
 *
 * EVERYTHING FROM HERE DOWN IS HISTORY, kept because a superseded measurement is
 * a record of a choice where a silently updated one is a rewrite of it.
 *
 * SiteHeader — grouped primary nav (lg+). The mobile bar takes over below lg.
 *
 * Why grouped: the site outgrew a flat bar. Ten flat links leave EXACTLY 0px of
 * slack at the lg breakpoint (1024px) — measured — and visibly break there: the
 * wordmark collides with the first link and three items wrap. Grouping Bio /
 * Approach / Projects under "About" and Blog / AI News under "Writing" takes it
 * to seven roomy top-level items, and the space that frees is what lets
 * Confirmation Outlook (a hosted service with no /work/ row) and Review
 * Autopilot (the lead offer) both have a nav home. Nothing was demoted to get
 * here, and the top-level count must stay at seven.
 *
 * WORK AND SERVICES BECAME ONE SEAT ON 2026-09-11, at the operator's direction,
 * and the freed seat became "Compare". THE ROW HAS NO EIGHTH SEAT AND THAT IS
 * MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: at 1024px the container is 976px, seven seats use
 * 924px, and 52px is not enough for an eighth. A clone of an existing seat was
 * appended to the live row with six candidate labels — Reading, Compare,
 * Comparisons, What it costs, Costs, Readings — and every one of them wrapped,
 * including the five-letter one. So the three comparison sheets could only get
 * a nav home by a seat being given up, which is what happened.
 *
 * WHY THOSE TWO AND NOT ANOTHER PAIR. Work held three children that PROVE and
 * Services held two that SELL, and a visitor deciding between them was being
 * asked to know which of those two words meant "the things he makes". They are
 * one menu now, selling pages first. The merged seat keeps Work's label and
 * Work's position, because "Work" is the shorter word — which is what pays for
 * the new seat — and because D29's ordering note below still governs.
 *
 * Top-level ORDER is unchanged by D29 on purpose. Main Street is now the LOCAL
 * page — a narrower audience than before, not a wider one — so promoting it
 * above Work would bury the demo every visitor should try behind a page most
 * of them are not in the market for.
 *
 * THE CONTACT SEAT WAS GIVEN UP ON 2026-09-12 TO PAY FOR THE FULL MENU, at the
 * operator's direction, and the row is SIX seats plus a menu control. Everything
 * above about there being no eighth seat held exactly as written: the control
 * went in, `npm run measure:nav` reported WRAPPED at 1024px, and the header's own
 * rule — a top-level seat is only ever available by giving one up — was the
 * answer rather than a paragraph about it.
 *
 * THE THREE WAYS OUT WERE MEASURED, NOT ARGUED, and the numbers are the record
 * because they are what ruled the other two out. Slack in the HEADER CONTAINER
 * at 1024px, where the container is 976px:
 *
 *     seven seats, symbol-only control, button shaved to px-3      9px
 *     six seats, symbol-only control, everything else full size   64px
 *     six seats, the control carries the word "Menu"              16px   ← this
 *
 * THOSE SIXTEEN PIXELS ARE GONE AND THE ROW IS AT 9px, because the seats changed
 * again later the same day: "Compare" became the longer "Resources", About was
 * dissolved, and AI News took a seat. The reading above is kept because it is
 * what the Contact decision was made against — a superseded measurement is a
 * record of a choice, where a silently updated one is a rewrite of it. The
 * CURRENT number lives beside the Resources seat below, which is where the next
 * person changing a label will actually be reading.
 *
 * SIXTEEN PIXELS WAS NOT TIGHT BY THIS ROW'S STANDARDS. It is about what the row
 * carried before the menu existed, and the nine-pixel option bought its room by
 * narrowing the primary button and shrinking a click target, which is three
 * cosmetic compromises to buy one decision's worth of space.
 *
 * WHY CONTACT AND NOT ANOTHER SEAT. The "Start a project" button sits
 * immediately beside where that seat was and points at the same page, so the row
 * was carrying two controls for one destination — which is the duplication rule
 * this file already states about /ai-fit/. Contact is still reachable three
 * ways: that button, the full menu, and the phone bar's own tab.
 *
 * AND THE COST IS REAL, so it is written down rather than left for somebody to
 * rediscover. "Start a project" asks for more commitment than "Contact" does. A
 * visitor who only wants to ask a question may not press it, and if that turns
 * out to matter the fix is the button's LABEL rather than the seat coming back.
 *
 * Zero JS. The panels reveal on `group-hover` AND `group-focus-within`, and they
 * hide with `opacity-0 pointer-events-none` — NOT `invisible`. This is
 * load-bearing: `visibility: hidden` removes the child links from the tab order,
 * which would strand a keyboard user on a menu they can never open. Opacity keeps
 * them focusable, so tabbing into one reveals its panel.
 */
import Wordmark from './Wordmark.astro';
import NavIcon from './NavIcon.astro';
import { Menu as MenuIcon, X } from '@lucide/astro';
import { dest, menu, assertSiteMap, cta, ctaHref } from '../data/site-map.js';

assertSiteMap('SiteHeader');

const path = Astro.url.pathname;

/* THE BUTTON ASKS FOR WHAT THIS PAGE'S READER IS ACTUALLY DOING, since
   2026-09-12. It said "Start a project" on every page and pointed at a bare
   /contact/ — the same question on /bio/, where somebody is deciding whether
   this is a real person, as on /quoted/, where they are pricing work.

   THE RULE LIVES IN src/data/site-map.js AND NOT HERE. Every page is already in
   that file, an assertion there refuses an intent ContactForm.jsx would not
   recognize, and putting the map here would mean the one file that knows every
   page and the one that decides what to ask for are different files.

   A PAGE NOT IN THE MAP FALLS BACK RATHER THAN THROWING. `dest()` throws on an
   unknown page because a menu row pointing nowhere is a defect; a BUTTON is
   different — every layout renders this header, including error and utility
   routes that are deliberately not destinations, and a 404 that fails to render
   because its button had no rule would be a worse failure than a generic
   button. */
const pageCta = cta(path);

/* A SEAT IS A LABEL AND AN ADDRESS, since 2026-09-20. The heading, child and
   dropdown machinery that used to live here went with the dropdowns; what a
   page is CALLED still comes from src/data/site-map.js, and the seat label is a
   measured shortening typed here, as it always was. */
type Entry = { label: string; href: string };

const nav: Entry[] = [
  /* THE LABELS ARE MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN, and the reading is kept here. Run
     `npm run measure:nav` with a preview on 4301 and read the HEADER CONTAINER
     line for 1024px. "For manufacturers" is the longest label this row has
     ever carried; if it ever wraps, the fallback the operator agreed is the
     shorter "Manufacturers", never an eighth pixel squeezed from the button.

     MEASURED 2026-09-20, the day these five landed: at 1024px the container is
     976px, the row uses 874px, slack 102px, one row; 178px at 1100px and 230px
     at 1280px. The six-seat row this replaced had 9px. */
  { label: 'Websites', href: '/websites/' },
  { label: 'Main Street', href: '/main-street/' },
  { label: 'For manufacturers', href: '/supply-chain/' },
  { label: 'Bio', href: '/bio/' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '/contact/' },
];

const isActive = (href: string) =>
  path === href || path.startsWith(href.endsWith('/') ? href : href + '/');
---
{/* The frosted blur is DESKTOP-ONLY (`lg:`). A sticky header carrying a
    `backdrop-filter` sits pinned over scrolling content, so the browser
    re-rasterizes whatever is behind it on EVERY scroll frame — on a phone that is a
    top cause of choppy scrolling. On mobile it goes near-solid instead: visually
    near-identical at that size (the net barely reads behind a 95% bar anyway) and
    free to composite.

    The blur is a Tailwind UTILITY, so it CANNOT be turned off from @layer components
    — v4 puts utilities in a later layer, which wins regardless of specificity. It has
    to be made conditional here, in the markup. */}
<header
  class="sticky top-0 z-50 border-b border-line bg-canvas/95 lg:bg-canvas/80 lg:backdrop-blur-md lg:supports-[backdrop-filter]:bg-canvas/65"
>
  <div class="mx-auto flex h-16 max-w-6xl items-center justify-between px-6">
    <Wordmark />
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <nav class="hidden items-center gap-0.5 lg:flex" aria-label="Primary">
        {
          nav.map((entry) => {
            const active = isActive(entry.href);
            return (
              <a
                href={entry.href}
                aria-current={active ? 'page' : undefined}
                class={`relative inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition-colors duration-200 ${
                  active ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink-muted hover:text-ink'
                }`}
              >
                {entry.label}
                {active && (
                  <span
                    class="absolute bottom-1 left-1/2 h-px w-4 -translate-x-1/2 bg-accent"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  />
                )}
              </a>
            );
          })
        }
      </nav>
      {/* ── THE FULL MENU, 2026-09-12 ──────────────────────────────────────
          The phone has carried a complete menu since the tab bar was built —
          MobileTabBar's sheet, headed "Everything on this site". The desktop
          had seven seats with partial dropdowns and no full list at all, so
          /deadlines/ was reachable from no laptop menu whatsoever.

          IT SITS OUTSIDE <nav> AND THAT IS NOT A DETAIL. The seven-seat row is
          FULL: six candidate eighth labels were clone-tested in the live row at
          1024px on 2026-09-11 and every one wrapped, including a five-letter
          one. This control lives in the right-hand cluster beside the button,
          where the phone's Menu control also lives, so the measured row is
          untouched and `npm run measure:nav` still reads the same slack.

          A <details> RATHER THAN A HOVER PANEL, matching the phone. The header
          comment above warns that `visibility: hidden` strands a keyboard user
          on a menu they can never open, and a closed <details> hides its
          content the same way — but the two are not the same case. There the
          trigger was a hover with nothing focusable to press; here <summary> is
          a real, focusable control that opens the panel when activated. That is
          what a disclosure is, and it carries native semantics for a screen
          reader with no script at all.

          THE PANEL IS `absolute`, NEVER `fixed`. The header carries
          `lg:backdrop-blur-md`, and a backdrop filter makes an element the
          containing block for its `position: fixed` descendants — the same trap
          MobileTabBar records, where the phone sheet resolved against the 64px
          bar and opened invisibly. `absolute` resolves against the sticky
          header, which is what this wants anyway. */}
      <details class="group/menu hidden lg:block">
        <summary
          class="menu-summary flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2.5 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-ink-muted transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-ink"
          aria-label="Everything on this site"
          title="Everything on this site"
        >
          <MenuIcon size={17} class="group-open/menu:hidden" aria-hidden="true" />
          <X size={17} class="hidden group-open/menu:block" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span>Menu</span>
        </summary>

        <div
          class="absolute inset-x-0 top-full border-b border-line bg-canvas shadow-2xl shadow-black/10"
        >
          <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-9 pt-7">
            <p class="mono-label text-cyan-deep">Everything on this site</p>

            {/* Home leads, above the columns rather than inside one. Its group
                carries no heading — there is no honest heading for a single
                row, and a lone link under one of the others would be filed
                somewhere it does not belong. */}
            {
              menu()
                .filter((g) => g.title === null)
                .flatMap((g) => g.items)
                .map((d) => (
                  <a
                    href={d.href}
                    aria-current={isActive(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                    class={`mt-4 flex items-baseline gap-3 border-b border-line pb-4 transition-colors ${
                      isActive(d.href) ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink-muted hover:text-ink'
                    }`}
                  >
                    <span class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{d.label}</span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-ink-faint">{d.note}</span>
                  </a>
                ))
            }

            {/* CSS COLUMNS RATHER THAN A GRID, because the groups are seven,
                three and two rows long and a grid row is as tall as its tallest
                cell — which would open gaps of empty space under the short
                ones. Columns pack them. `break-inside-avoid` is what stops a
                group being split across a column boundary, and without it the
                heading and its rows come apart. */}
            <div class="mt-6 gap-x-10 lg:columns-3">
              {
                menu()
                  .filter((g) => g.title !== null)
                  .map((g) => (
                    <div class="mb-7 break-inside-avoid">
                      <p class="mono-label pb-2 text-cyan-deep">{g.title}</p>
                      <ul class="flex flex-col gap-0.5">
                        {g.items.map((d) => (
                          <li>
                            <a
                              href={d.href}
                              aria-current={isActive(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                              class={`flex items-start gap-3 rounded-md px-2 py-1.5 transition-colors ${
                                isActive(d.href)
                                  ? 'bg-surface-2 text-ink'
                                  : 'text-ink-muted hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-ink'
                              }`}
                            >
                              <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-accent-2">
                                <NavIcon name={d.icon} size={15} />
                              </span>
                              <span class="min-w-0">
                                <span class="block text-sm font-medium">{d.label}</span>
                                <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-ink-faint">{d.note}</span>
                              </span>
                            </a>
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  ))
              }
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      {
        pageCta && (
          <a href={ctaHref(pageCta)} class="btn btn-primary inline-flex px-4 py-2 text-sm">
            {pageCta.label}
          </a>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</header>
